oleaut32: do not round the last half second of year 9999 into year 10000

VarUdateFromDate() rounds to the nearest second and rolls the date forward.
For a DATE in the last half second of 31 Dec 9999 (DateTime.MaxValue is
2958465.9999999884) VariantTimeToSystemTime() therefore succeeds with
1 Jan 10000, which SystemTimeToVariantTime() rejects.
Analysis Services round-trips a trace StopTime through both functions.
Desktop sends the maximum date at every start, so the engine raised a system
error, crashed while unwinding and launched SQLDUMPER, whose console window
appeared beside Desktop.
Keep 23:59:59 for that half second so that the result stays a valid date.
The Windows result for this exact value was not measured; the working round
trip in Analysis Services on Windows shows that it returns a valid date.
Wine-derived change: LGPL-2.1-or-later.

--- a/dlls/oleaut32/variant.c
+++ b/dlls/oleaut32/variant.c
@@ -1456,7 +1456,11 @@
   lpUdate->st.wSecond = timePart;
   timePart -= lpUdate->st.wSecond;
   lpUdate->st.wMilliseconds = 0;
-  if (timePart > 0.5)
+  /* Rounding the last half second of 31 Dec 9999 up would produce year 10000,
+   * which no date function accepts back. Keep 23:59:59 there. */
+  if (timePart > 0.5 &&
+      !(lpUdate->st.wYear == 9999 && lpUdate->st.wMonth == 12 && lpUdate->st.wDay == 31 &&
+        lpUdate->st.wHour == 23 && lpUdate->st.wMinute == 59 && lpUdate->st.wSecond == 59))
   {
     /* Round the milliseconds, adjusting the time/date forward if needed */
     if (lpUdate->st.wSecond < 59)
